A man in his early 60s with a history of prostate adenocarcinoma (initially staged T3N0M0, Gleason score 9 in 2015) treated with radiation therapy and long-term androgen-deprivation therapy presented for evaluation of biochemical recurrence. His PSA level had risen to 25 ng/mL in 2021. He was asymptomatic at the time of referral.  

An ^18F-PSMA-1007 PET/CT scan was obtained to assess the extent of recurrence and dissemination. Findings included:  
• On CT, nodular tissue growth in the right side of the prostate;  
• A blind-ending tubular structure arising from the cecum (the appendix) that was enlarged compared to a prior CT 1 year earlier, with multiple soft-tissue nodules and associated periappendiceal fat stranding;  
• Nodular soft-tissue lesions adjacent to the umbilicus and posterior to the bladder.  
On PET imaging, there was increased ^18F-PSMA uptake in the right prostate, bilateral external iliac lymph nodes, mesenteric lymph nodes, and in the lesions at the appendix, umbilicus, and posterior bladder wall.

A 39-year-old man with a history of kidney stones and Bell’s palsy was referred for an endocrine consultation before a third planned parathyroid exploration. Three months earlier he had been hospitalized with presumed Guillain–Barré syndrome because of progressive bilateral upper- and lower-extremity weakness, numbness, and facial paresthesia. He received 12 sessions of plasmapheresis, required insulin for hyperglycemia, and was treated with pregabalin and carbamazepine (later switched to duloxetine), with partial improvement of sensory symptoms.

On repeat evaluation, laboratory tests showed:
• Calcium, 19.3 mg/dl;  
• Parathyroid hormone (PTH), 185 pg/ml;  
• Vitamin D, 14.2 ng/ml;  
• Phosphorus, 3.9 mg/dl;  
• Hematuria on urinalysis.  

Serum and urine protein electrophoreses and a bone densitometry test were normal. A 99mTc-sestamibi parathyroid SPECT-CT and thyroid uptake scan demonstrated a thyroid nodule but no parathyroid adenoma; fine-needle aspiration of the nodule was benign. He underwent two parathyroid surgeries for presumed hyperplasia, but pathology specimens were normal. Treatment with cinacalcet and nasal calcitonin normalized calcium and vitamin D levels, yet PTH remained >100 pg/ml. 

Because of persistent hematuria, proteinuria (1.2 g in 24 hours), and a creatinine level of 2.9 mg/dl, systemic lupus erythematosus, vasculitis, antiphospholipid syndrome, and hepatitis were considered but excluded. A renal biopsy revealed extensive effacement of visceral foot processes without immune deposits (minimal change disease vs unsampled focal and segmental glomerulosclerosis), and his renal function improved after cyclophosphamide and corticosteroids. Abdominal and pelvic ultrasound showed a 20.4-mm right inguinal lymph node but no organomegaly.

Four months later, he was readmitted with severe pain in his hands and feet, paresthesia, difficulty walking, and edema. Thoracic CT and hip MRI revealed bilateral femoral-head avascular necrosis; a well-defined enhancing mass (55 × 50 mm) at the left T11–T12 paravertebral region was excised and identified as a ganglioneuroma. Whole-body bone scintigraphy showed lucencies in the pubic symphysis and right ischium, suggestive of bone-marrow fibrosis and plasma cell–rich infiltration.
